{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqfgcwu5yqi","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/zonoid","title":"context injection bench verdict: MCP search access drives KB task improvement, text prefix injection is noise or harmful","summary":"5-arm sonnet bench (10 scenarios, N=1 per cell) on mix-set suite. Arms: arm0=no-MCP-no-text, arm4=MCP-only-no-text, arm1=MCP+global-summary-text, arm2=MCP+sliding-window-text, arm3=MCP+both-text.\n\nKey finding on rate-limiter-strategy (only reliable discriminator): arm0 TIMEOUT, arm4 PASS, arm1 TIMEOUT, arm2 PASS, arm3 TIMEOUT. MCP access alone (arm4) matches the best outcome. Pre-injected global-summary text (orchestrator task status summary) causes timeouts — actively harmful. Sliding-window text is neutral.\n\ncache-eviction-policy was not a useful discriminator — sonnet guesses CLOCK semantics without KB access (all 5 arms pass).\n\nInterpretation: search_knowledge KB retrieval is the load-bearing mechanism for KB-required tasks. Injecting irrelevant orchestrator status text as a prompt prefix adds noise that hurts performance. Production workers (MCP-on by default) already operate at arm4 level. The context injection arms (global-summary, sliding-window) provide no benefit over MCP-only and should NOT be wired into production dispatch.\n\nMix-set bench is concluded. Next: FeatureBench (FB-2) for statistically credible measurement on real-world repo tasks.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-15T16:53:39.773Z","vec":[-0.021870827302336693,0.044982556253671646,0.0353262796998024,0.0023592764046043158,-0.027443528175354004,0.001891530235297978,0.05183388665318489,0.014731650240719318,0.011515703983604908,0.027904586866497993,-0.030657175928354263,-0.0002676973817870021,0.08920656144618988,-0.06377799063920975,0.012621227651834488,0.008684760890901089,0.21493157744407654,-0.05798927694559097,-0.03247469291090965,0.026269223541021347,0.034417830407619476,-0.03234105557203293,0.007731987163424492,-0.0002487056772224605,-0.0293505433946